Part 1:
1. Go to the Digilent Vivado Library on GitHub: https://github.com/Digilent/vivado-library
2. Click the green Code button and select Download ZIP.
3. Extract the ZIP file on your local computer.
4. Move the extracted folder to a permanent, easy-to-find location. (Putting it directly inside your main Vivado project folder and renaming it to ip_repo makes it easy to find).

Part 2:
1. Open your project in Vivado.
2. On the left side of the screen in the Flow Navigator, click the Settings gear icon (or go to Tools > Settings in the top menu).
3. In the Settings window, expand the IP category on the left pane and click on Repository.
4. Click the + (Plus) button to add a new repository.
5. Browse to the ip_repo folder you extracted in Part 1, select it, and click Select.
6. A small window will pop up showing the number of IPs Vivado found inside that folder. Click OK, then click Apply and OK to close the Settings window

Part 3:
1. Click Add Sources in the Flow Navigator.
2. Select Add Existing IP and browse to the .xci files provided in this repository (e.g., rgb2dvi_0.xci and clk_wiz_0.xci). Click Finish.
3. In the Sources pane, expand the IP Sources or Hierarchy tab. You should see the custom IPs listed.
4. Right-click the IPs and select Generate Output Products.
5. Leave the settings default in the pop-up window and click Generate. Vivado will take a few moments to build the background files for the IP.

Part 4:
1. Click Add Sources in the Flow Navigator.
2. Select Add or create design sources and click Next.
3. Click Add Files and select the provided DisplayHDMI.v file (and your image module Verilog files). Click Finish.
4. Click Add Sources again, select Add or create constraints, and click Next.
5. Click Add Files and select the BooleanBoard.xdc file to ensure the physical pins are mapped correctly.
